module.exports = {
  A1: `During the preceding three years, the school's formal preparation for collaborative professional learning included an introduction to an improvement cycle organized around planning, trying a change, studying evidence, and deciding what to do next. Administrators introduced this process last year as part of a schoolwide effort to improve reading comprehension in English language arts. In a professional development session, they explained how a common inquiry process could help teams examine learning and coordinate instruction. The initial preparation was brief, but it gave grade levels a shared way to organize their work.

The school created one slide presentation containing templates that every grade level used. Teams recorded a problem of practice, selected a strategy, identified evidence, and analyzed student learning in that shared space. Because the presentation was available schoolwide, teachers could see other teams' questions, instructional choices, and findings. Grade-level members completed the process together rather than submit separate individual plans. At several points during the year, teams presented results to colleagues. This helped establish common language for discussing reading comprehension and made the relationship between collaborative inquiry and the schoolwide goal more visible.

Current collaboration takes place through several established arrangements. Schoolwide professional development occurs two or three times a month and brings together teachers, specialists, coordinators, and administrators. Sessions may involve reviewing school priorities, examining iReady and DIBELS results, preparing for state assessments, or clarifying curriculum expectations. These activities can support a shared understanding of what students should learn and more consistent access to instruction. Their value depends partly on how much time remains for examining teaching and learning after other business is addressed.

Teachers also meet with their grade-level colleagues every other week during the instructional day. They discuss recent data, student work, and pacing. Those conversations can lead to prompt reteaching or changes in instruction when a need becomes apparent. The school offers up to two additional hours of optional after-school collaboration each month. Participation in that time varies because teachers' schedules differ. Consequently, some teams benefit from more shared planning than others, and the effects of that additional time are uneven across the school.

More structured data conversations are beginning to develop, but their use is not yet consistent. Collaboration could help staff recognize academic needs early and coordinate support for English learners and students receiving intervention. Logistical requirements can also limit that potential. Teams are expected to meet when all members are available, which means some groups have difficulty meeting at all while others engage in sustained inquiry. Paraprofessionals are not regularly included in these arrangements. Their absence from planning can weaken the connection between classroom instruction and the additional support students receive.

The school's history of professional learning has nevertheless established an expectation that teachers work together. Regular professional development and grade-level meetings make discussion of data, instruction, and pacing part of normal professional practice. These routines have helped coordinate classrooms and given teachers recurring opportunities to learn from one another. They provide a foundation for a community of practice because collaboration is familiar rather than entirely new.

The quality of that collaboration varies. Sessions centered on logistics leave less time for exploring an instructional problem. Optional meeting time and the requirement for full attendance can make participation uneven. The common inquiry process introduced last year strengthened the work by giving teams a specific sequence and a shared place to document evidence. Viewing one another's results increased transparency and supported alignment. Professional learning therefore helps collaboration when it gives educators time and structure to investigate instruction, while arrangements emphasizing compliance or restricting participation can limit its usefulness.

Several adult-learning principles are visible in these practices. Teachers work with current assessment information and instructional questions that arise in their classrooms. This reflects Knowles' account of adult learning as relevant to professional responsibilities and organized around problems adults need to address (1984). The immediate connection to students gives the work a practical purpose and allows teachers to draw on their existing experience.

The meetings also illustrate learning through social participation. In the traditions associated with Vygotsky and with Lave and Wenger, understanding develops through interaction and shared activity. Teachers compare interpretations, solve problems together, and build collective expertise. Finally, the inquiry cycle includes collaboration, attention to student outcomes, and continuing reflection, features discussed by Darling-Hammond and colleagues in their work on effective professional learning. Not every session reflects these principles equally. The strongest alignment appears when educators examine evidence together, make an instructional decision, try it, and return to consider what students' responses suggest.`,
  A2: `Two conditions need attention as I prepare to facilitate this team. The first is reliable participation. Even within one grade level, attendance changes with the meeting arrangement and time of day. When members are missing, the group loses perspectives needed for a shared interpretation of student learning. Decisions may then feel less collective, and the team may need to repeat discussions before moving forward. I will need to establish arrangements that make sustained participation feasible.

The second condition is a clear inquiry routine. Existing meetings sometimes rely on informal discussion and move from instruction into scheduling or other logistics. I will need protocols that bring the team back to evidence, help members examine student responses, and support reflection on the strategies they try. Consistent structures can make the difference between meeting regularly and learning together. My facilitation should help the group use its time to reach shared, evidence-informed decisions about instruction and subsequent adjustments.`,
  A3: `The educational focus is mathematics, particularly progress toward proficiency on the Smarter Balanced Assessment. Administration and I selected this focus after examining school SBA trends and district priorities. The district expects students in grades three through five to move an average of 40 points nearer proficiency by 2026, with a 50-point gain expected for targeted groups. Our school has shown continuing growth and currently scores above the proficiency threshold in mathematics. However, its latest Distance from Standard value of 43.7 remains below the district's projected 2025-2026 target, so further acceleration is needed. Mathematics therefore provides a relevant focus even in a school with positive overall results. The work also connects with the mission of equitable access to rigorous teaching and with the district's longer-term intention that students be prepared for more advanced mathematics, including Algebra.`,
  A4: `The community includes three third-grade teachers. One teaches an SAS class with a broad range of learner needs. Her perspective can help the group choose mathematics approaches that remain useful across different levels of readiness while supporting the SBA focus.

A second SAS teacher contributes experience with enrichment and accelerated instruction. As the grade-level chair, she can also help coordinate the team's work and maintain an appropriate level of challenge across classrooms.

The general education teacher represents another range of instructional levels within third grade. His knowledge of scaffolds and additional support will help the group consider how students can gain access to the mathematics proficiency goal.

Third grade is the first year students take the mathematics SBA. Working with this team offers an opportunity to strengthen that transition and, if the collaboration proves useful, extend its approach to the upper grades as the school pursues the district numeracy targets.

I invited the teachers during a grade-level meeting by explaining the community's purpose and its relationship to the schoolwide mathematics goal. Each agreed to participate. They saw the work as an opportunity to improve mathematics teaching, support students approaching their first SBA, and develop greater instructional consistency across the grade level.`,
  A5: `The two SAS teachers and the general education teacher bring different backgrounds to the group. One SAS teacher identifies as Hispanic, connecting with the approximately 59% of students identified as Latino, the school's largest demographic group. The other SAS teacher identifies as White, a group comprising about 16% of enrollment. The general education teacher is Armenian. Armenian identity is not listed separately in the school's demographic chart, although many families in the community share that background. His perspective therefore relates to a substantial community presence that is less visible in the chart's reporting categories.`,
  A6: `I teach second grade at the same school and participate as a colleague of the third-grade team. My students enter their grade next, so I bring knowledge of the foundational skills and instructional experiences that precede third-grade mathematics. That position allows me to contribute to discussion of the transition between grades and the preparation students receive. I do not supervise or evaluate these teachers. My role is that of a peer and facilitator, working alongside them to examine evidence and improve mathematics learning rather than directing their performance from an administrative position.`,
  A7: `The community's intended benefit is more consistent and responsive mathematics instruction across third grade. By examining student evidence together, teachers can identify numeracy needs and select approaches that strengthen the foundational skills required for SBA success. Joint planning and reflection should help establish coherent routines, shared expectations, and suitable supports for learners with different starting points. Students may then experience clearer instruction and greater confidence in mathematics, contributing to learning and well-being.

The group also creates a continuing process for revisiting those decisions. Teachers can bring new evidence, compare how students respond, and modify instruction when needs change. Shared analysis can strengthen their understanding of effective practice and distribute responsibility for student progress across the team. Over time, that process may support more equitable learning opportunities by making instructional choices deliberate and responsive. These are expected benefits to investigate through implementation and student evidence, rather than outcomes already demonstrated before the community begins its work.`,
  B: `Meeting date: October 13, 2025. Meeting time: 1:30-2:30 p.m.

Working agreements: Give the discussion full attention; arrive prepared to contribute; consider perspectives different from one's own; and keep the conversation focused on the shared purpose. These agreements will guide both participation and collaborative decision-making throughout the meeting.

Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and grade-level chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher serving as facilitator.

Student focus: Third-grade learners, with particular attention to foundational numeracy needs identified in beginning-of-year iReady results.

Meeting purpose: Examine available assessment information, including SBA and iReady, establish a shared instructional problem, and select one evidence-informed approach to support third-grade number sense.

School and district connection: Revisit the district goal for grades three through five to move an average of 40 points toward SBA proficiency by 2026. Connect this target with the school's commitment to rigorous instruction that is accessible to all learners. Use iReady growth expectations as ongoing information about progress toward the larger mathematics goals.

Evidence for review: Third-grade iReady beginning-of-year overall placement; iReady domain results for Number and Operations, Algebraic Thinking, and Measurement and Data; and mathematics SBA Distance from Standard trends.

Data discussion: Compare overall placement with domain strengths and areas needing support. Identify patterns that may explain difficulty accessing grade-level standards, especially foundational skills needed for later concepts. Invite teachers to connect the assessment information with what they observe in classrooms. Use the discussion to move toward a problem the whole team recognizes.

Problem of practice: Define an instructional concern that is specific enough to guide action, supported by the evidence, and within teachers' influence. Focus on how instruction can improve access to grade-level mathematics rather than describe students only in terms of low scores. Clarify which foundational needs the first cycle should address.

Strategy discussion: Consider more than one research-informed approach. Compare each option with the identified need, the range of student readiness, and the realities of classroom implementation. Reach a shared decision on one primary strategy after members have had an opportunity to discuss its fit.

Implementation planning: Outline the routines, group arrangements, common materials, and monitoring tools needed to use the selected strategy across classrooms. Agree on how the team will coordinate the work while remaining responsive to individual students. Establish an initial timeline for reviewing evidence and discussing progress.

Closing decisions: Record responsibilities, resources to prepare, evidence to collect, and the next collaboration date. Confirm what each person will bring back so the following meeting can build directly on the decisions made today.`,
  C: `The meeting opened by connecting the inquiry with the school's commitment to rigorous, accessible instruction. Teachers discussed how strong early mathematics understanding prepares students for more advanced work later. We also revisited the district expectation that students in grades three through five move an average of 40 points closer to SBA proficiency by 2026. Alongside this longer-term target, members considered the importance of meeting iReady typical and stretch growth expectations during the year. The group agreed that strengthening number sense was a meaningful contribution to both the school mission and district priorities.

I then guided the team through the third-grade beginning-of-year iReady results. We began with overall placement, where relatively few students were at or above grade level and most were one or more grades below. Moving to the domain results, we identified notable needs in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. I asked members to consider how these areas support addition and subtraction with regrouping, a major third-grade focus and a foundation for later mathematics. This sequence helped the group move from a broad performance pattern toward a more specific instructional concern.

To connect assessment reports with classroom experience, I invited teachers to share early work samples and observations. Several described difficulty with place value and regrouping. Others noted students who already showed strong conceptual understanding and needed additional challenge. I summarized this range so that our planning would address both groups. The discussion made clear that a single uniform task would not necessarily provide appropriate access for everyone. Students needing foundational instruction and students ready for enrichment were present within the same grade level and, often, within the same classroom.

The team considered what this range meant for its practice. I prompted members to think about how more deliberate planning and greater consistency across classrooms could support different needs. We agreed that instruction would need to be focused enough to accelerate progress while allowing teachers to respond to students' readiness. I closed the data discussion by restating the shared concern: we needed a coordinated approach that could provide differentiated opportunities without losing coherence across the grade. This understanding became the basis for our problem of practice and strategy discussion.

The group framed its problem as a need for more consistent, targeted early numeracy instruction because gaps in foundational number sense were limiting students' access to grade-level mathematics. The intended improvement was progress toward iReady growth expectations and SBA proficiency. This wording connected student evidence with an instructional responsibility the team could address. It also made the problem more actionable than a general statement that mathematics scores needed to rise.

In developing that statement, I returned the group to beginning-of-year placement, domain results, and classroom work. Teachers identified number and operations concepts that underpin addition and subtraction with regrouping. I asked how difficulty with those concepts affects participation in grade-level work and may contribute to continuing performance differences. Members also emphasized that the problem could not be addressed by planning only for students below grade level. Students performing at or above expectations needed purposeful opportunities to extend their reasoning. We therefore identified grouping and targeted practice as aspects of instruction that required reconsideration.

The team agreed that this problem was sufficiently focused, relevant to school and district goals, and within our instructional influence. Addressing it would require intentional changes in how teachers organize learning, reinforce foundational concepts, and provide enrichment. We wanted students to receive appropriately demanding mathematics rather than equate differentiation with easier work for some and additional work for others. The problem statement gave us a shared basis for comparing possible strategies.

I introduced two research-informed options for discussion. The first was flexible grouping based on current skill needs, using the NCSM resource Supporting All Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices. I highlighted temporary placement, movement as students develop, and targeted teaching without permanent tracking. I asked how that approach could serve students needing foundational support as well as students ready for enrichment. Teachers recognized that their range of readiness made a flexible structure relevant. They also discussed the importance of using current evidence when assigning or changing groups.

The second option was a daily mathematics reasoning routine, drawing on Heinemann's Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. We considered how Number Talks could develop conceptual understanding, number sense, and mathematical discussion. Structured opportunities to explain reasoning may be particularly helpful for English learners and students who need support expressing their thinking. Teachers saw how these routines could complement the core program by making students' strategies and language more visible. This comparison gave the group more than one plausible response to the identified problem.

After discussing the evidence and classroom conditions, the team selected flexible, skill-based grouping as the primary strategy. Members felt it offered the most direct way to provide different levels of support and challenge across the grade. We used the local name Math Crew for the approach, while identifying the underlying strategy as the flexible grouping model discussed by NCSM. The name was a convenient way to refer to the work, not a separate evidence base. Teachers wanted groups that could address place value and regrouping for some students while providing more advanced problem-solving opportunities for others.

I next facilitated an initial implementation plan. We narrowed the first cycle to addition and subtraction with regrouping so that the work would be manageable and coordinated. Both the assessment information and classroom observations supported this choice. During the meeting, teachers developed a short preassessment of place value and regrouping concepts. They agreed to administer it before the next meeting and use the results to make grouping decisions. This meant that group membership would follow evidence about the selected skills rather than broad impressions of student ability.

We discussed what teaching within the groups might involve. Some students would work on composing and decomposing numbers, relationships within place value, and concrete or visual representations of regrouping. Others would engage in extension and more complex problem solving. I emphasized that the groups should remain temporary and change as students' understanding develops. The purpose was to provide an appropriate next learning opportunity, not assign a lasting label to a student. Members agreed that the preassessment and subsequent work would help determine where each student should begin and when a change was appropriate.

For consistency, teachers agreed to protect a common instructional period for Math Crew and use shared language, routines, and visual models. We also planned to exchange resources and collaborate on lessons. This would support alignment while preserving teachers' ability to respond to the learners in front of them. The duration of the first cycle would be decided after reviewing the preassessment. A postassessment would then provide information about progress and help the team determine subsequent instruction. These arrangements linked the strategy with a process for studying its implementation rather than treating selection as the end of planning.

At the close, we confirmed responsibilities and the next meeting's purpose. Teachers would administer and score the regrouping preassessment, then bring results, observations, and questions about grouping or instruction. Together we would finalize groups, determine the length of the first cycle, and identify or adapt resources for each group. We would also plan the postassessment used to examine progress. My continuing role would be to help members notice patterns, compare interpretations, coordinate classroom expectations, and make shared adjustments. The next steps kept the inquiry connected to evidence and to the practical decisions needed to improve access to grade-level mathematics.`,
  D1: `At the planning meeting, I organized the review around several sources related to third-grade mathematics. We began with beginning-of-year iReady results and the SBA numeracy trend so everyone had the same starting information. I asked teachers to examine overall placement, including the share of students below grade level, before moving to individual domains. Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking showed particular areas of need. I invited members to compare these results across classrooms and describe what they were seeing during instruction, giving the assessment reports a more specific classroom context.

To develop the analysis, I asked the team to identify patterns that appeared across the grade and consider which prerequisite skills were making grade-level mathematics difficult. These prompts helped connect scores with observable learning. Teachers described students' difficulty composing and decomposing numbers, understanding place value, and using regrouping. They explained how those difficulties affected participation in current tasks rather than simply repeat the assessment labels.

I also made space for evidence about students who were already meeting or exceeding expectations. Members recognized that these learners needed enrichment while classmates needed foundational support. Bringing both needs into the discussion helped us avoid defining the problem only around remediation. The group developed a shared problem of practice centered on more deliberate, consistent instruction in foundational number sense. We concluded that this would require changes in the organization of mathematics instruction, particularly grouping and the reinforcement of prerequisite skills. Those changes would need to work across classrooms and support progress toward both iReady growth expectations and the SBA goal.`,
  D2: `I helped the team extend its review to demographic and performance information, including English learners, students with disabilities, and students without identified support needs. The beginning-of-year iReady results showed many students below grade level in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. Teachers described similar concerns in several classrooms and across groups, suggesting a shared instructional issue rather than a difficulty confined to one class.

We then compared those patterns with student work and daily observations. Members described limited understanding of place value and regrouping, explaining how these gaps made grade-level tasks less accessible. They also identified students with stronger understanding who needed opportunities for extension. This comparison made the range of readiness within each classroom clearer. It supported planning for both foundational instruction and enrichment, while keeping the discussion connected to actual student responses rather than relying on demographic categories alone.`,
  D3: `I asked the team to examine whether its current arrangements allowed teachers to respond to the range of readiness we had identified. We considered which teaching practices could address differing starting points more deliberately and how changes in grouping might bring instruction closer to students' particular skill needs. These questions shifted the conversation from describing performance to considering what adults could change. Teachers recognized that the problem called for adjustments in the way mathematics instruction was organized and delivered, especially the support provided within groups.

We agreed to frame the problem as a need for more consistent and targeted early numeracy teaching because gaps in foundational number sense were limiting access to grade-level mathematics. The intended direction was improved progress toward iReady growth expectations and SBA proficiency. This statement connected the student need with an instructional response within the team's influence and gave members a common purpose for the next stage of planning.`,
  D4: `The problem of practice connects with the school's commitment to rigorous learning opportunities that are accessible to every student and prepare them for advanced mathematics. Strengthening number sense also supports the district's longer-term numeracy priorities. It is consistent with the California Mathematics Framework's attention to conceptual understanding, numerical fluency, and flexible approaches to solving problems.

The focus responds to performance differences visible in third grade. Foundational needs appeared across several student groups, and students below grade level would need accelerated progress to meet annual growth expectations. Addressing those needs can improve access to current instruction. At the same time, the team must provide continued challenge for students already on track so that the strategy advances learning across the full range of readiness.`,
  D5: `After the team established its instructional problem, I introduced two research-informed possibilities. One was temporary grouping by skill, supported by NCSM's Supporting All Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices. The other was a daily reasoning routine such as Number Talks, using Heinemann's Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. We discussed what each would look like in practice and compared its fit with students' needs, implementation demands, and opportunities to differentiate across readiness levels.

I supported the decision by asking members to consider which option most directly addressed the addition and subtraction needs in the data, which could serve the range of learners, and which we could use consistently across classrooms. These questions gave the comparison shared criteria rather than leave the choice to individual preference. Teachers noted that flexible grouping would allow them to reorganize students around current skills, teach particular concepts more directly, and offer enrichment to students who already demonstrated proficiency.

The group reached agreement on flexible grouping, which we called Math Crew locally. We selected it because it appeared to offer the best fit for foundational numeracy needs while supporting differentiated teaching for all learners. The local name refers to our implementation of the strategy; the research basis remains the flexible grouping approach discussed in the resource.`,
  D6: `Teachers chose flexible grouping because their classes include students well below, at, and above grade-level expectations. Temporary groups organized around current skills seemed a practical way to provide more precisely matched instruction. Members also knew that kindergarten through second grade had used similar arrangements in English language arts. That experience offered a local model for responding to varied learning needs. Because I had experience with that work, I could help the team, and members could also consult K-2 colleagues who had used flexible groups extensively.

During the meeting, we created a short preassessment to guide Math Crew placement. It addresses addition and subtraction with regrouping and the underlying place-value understanding those operations require. Teachers agreed to administer and score it before the next community meeting. We would then use the results to determine groups and instructional priorities rather than assign students solely from a broad assessment level. Members also discussed resources, manipulatives, routines, and possible teaching approaches. They expressed interest in observing one another during implementation to learn how the groups functioned in different classrooms.

We anticipated several practical challenges. The team would need a dependable instructional period, a process for moving students as they progressed, and enough alignment that group experiences remained coherent across classrooms. Teachers agreed to use common routines and language and to check in regularly during subsequent meetings. Those arrangements would help preserve flexibility without making the work unmanageable. They would also provide opportunities to notice when a group or lesson needed adjustment. The intention was a responsive system in which current evidence informs placement and instruction, supported by collaboration rather than fixed group assignments for the duration of the year.`,
  D7: `The team expects more closely matched instruction to strengthen students' number sense, accuracy, and confidence with multidigit addition and subtraction. Students who find regrouping difficult would receive targeted support with the concepts underlying the procedure. Those already meeting or exceeding expectations would work in enrichment groups that extend reasoning and problem solving. In both cases, the goal is instruction suited to current readiness rather than the same task for everyone.

Teachers also anticipate greater engagement and less frustration during core mathematics lessons as students become more secure with prerequisite skills. More appropriate support and challenge may improve access to grade-level content and students' sense of competence. These are intended academic and well-being benefits. The group will need to examine student work and assessment results to determine which benefits appear and where further changes are needed.`,
  D8: `I will help the group establish a monitoring process that is clear enough to guide decisions and manageable enough to sustain. The shared preassessment will provide the starting evidence for Math Crew grouping. Teachers will administer and score it before our next meeting, when we will compare results and confirm placements together. This gives the team a common reference for discussing students' initial understanding.

During implementation, members will collect work samples, exit responses, observation notes, and informal checks focused on place value and regrouping. I will prompt the group to look for recurring patterns, identify students whose placement may need to change, and distinguish early progress from continuing difficulty. Each implementation meeting will begin with a brief review of this evidence. The purpose is to decide whether instruction or group arrangements need adjustment while the work is underway.

We also agreed to define an instructional period before administering a postassessment. Comparing the initial and later evidence will help us evaluate early changes in understanding. It will not by itself prove that grouping caused every change, but it will support a more informed discussion than impressions alone. Through shared analysis, regular check-ins, and reflection on classroom evidence, the team will monitor both the use of the strategy and students' responses to it.`,
  E1: `Implementation meeting 1: Third grade, November 5, 2025, 2:30-3:30 p.m.

Participation agreements: Attend to the discussion, arrive ready to contribute, consider alternative perspectives, and keep the conversation connected to the meeting purpose. The group will use these agreements while examining evidence and making decisions.

Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and grade-level chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher and facilitator.

Purpose: Review the initial use of Math Crew and the information emerging from the preassessment and classroom observations. Discuss how the strategy appears to support students with different levels of readiness in addition and subtraction with regrouping. Because implementation has only recently begun, the meeting will focus on questions and emerging patterns rather than final claims about effectiveness.

Initial evidence: Confirm whether teachers have completed the preassessment and invite their first observations about student responses. Compare patterns across classrooms and describe the variation in readiness within each class. Discuss early experiences organizing the groups, including student movement, routines, and expectations. Identify any questions about launching the strategy that need a shared response.

Discussion summary: Name the common patterns in students' understanding of regrouping. Identify promising features and difficulties in the initial rollout, including engagement, transitions, and coordination among teachers. Record questions that remain open or need more evidence. The facilitator will draw together the observations and ask members to confirm whether the summary represents their experience.

Actions before the next meeting: Continue temporary groups focused on addition and subtraction with regrouping. Refine expectations and procedures for moving between classrooms as needed. Gather further student work and observations during instruction so that the next discussion includes evidence of both learning and implementation. Identify practical supports or changes that would make the work more consistent.

Closing agreement: Confirm the focus of the next meeting and the information each teacher will bring. Clarify responsibilities for collecting evidence and following up on any shared concerns. The aim is to keep the first implementation cycle coordinated while allowing the team to respond to what it learns about students and the group structure.`,
  E2: `Implementation meeting 2: Third grade, November 19, 2025, 2:30-3:30 p.m.

Participation agreements: Give the meeting full attention, prepare to collaborate, remain receptive to different interpretations, and stay focused on the inquiry. These agreements support a discussion in which members can compare experiences and make shared instructional decisions.

Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and grade-level chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher facilitating the community.

Purpose: Reflect on the early Math Crew sessions, examine how students are responding within their current groups, and consider whether changes in teaching or placement would better meet their needs. The team will treat the available information as early implementation evidence rather than a final evaluation.

Evidence and experiences: Invite each teacher to describe recent sessions and students' responses. Look for indications of engagement, developing understanding, and persistent difficulty. Discuss whether particular students may need a different group and what evidence supports that possibility. Compare the instructional strategies being used at different levels and explain how lessons are being planned and adapted for each group.

Shared interpretation: Identify patterns that occur in more than one classroom. Note strengths in engagement and instruction, along with changes under consideration. Revisit whether the current focus and flexible grouping approach still fit the student needs identified in the inquiry. The facilitator will summarize the themes, connect related observations, and check whether members agree with the interpretation.

Next actions: Decide which proposed instructional changes or student moves should be tried. Continue with planned lessons for each group while monitoring progress and participation. Consider whether the current cycle needs additional time before the postassessment. Identify the work samples, assessment information, or observations that will be most useful for the following meeting.

Closing agreement: Confirm what each member will bring next time and identify any materials, coordination, or other support needed. Record the decisions so that teachers leave with the same understanding of the immediate plan. The meeting should result in specific actions that respond to students' current learning, while preserving the flexibility of the grouping arrangement as new evidence becomes available.`,
  E3: `Implementation meeting 3: Third grade, December 10, 2025, 2:30-3:30 p.m.

Participation agreements: Be attentive, prepare to contribute, consider other viewpoints, and maintain the instructional focus. Use these agreements to support shared interpretation of the evidence.

Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher and facilitator.

Purpose: Examine the Math Crew postassessment, identify progress and continuing needs, and agree on subsequent mathematics instruction across groups. The discussion will draw on results and observations collected over several weeks of implementation.

Evidence for discussion: Share each classroom's postassessment results and describe patterns of improvement, maintained performance, or unresolved difficulty. Examine regrouping, word problems, and prerequisite skills. Compare students' responses to the group structure and teaching, including differences among levels rather than relying only on an overall impression.

Synthesis: Identify patterns common to several classrooms and consider what they suggest about the current approach. Discuss features of implementation that appear useful, questions that remain unanswered, and areas needing refinement. The facilitator will connect observations across members and check that the group has a shared understanding of both progress and continuing instructional concerns.

Decisions and next steps: Consider changes in instruction suggested by the postassessment. Decide whether to extend the present cycle or revise its focus, and identify possible priorities for the next period of learning. Discuss how a future preassessment could target the relevant standards more precisely. Agree on the evidence or reflections members should bring to the next meeting.

The closing record will distinguish decisions already reached from questions requiring additional information. This will allow the team to continue the inquiry with a clear plan and to use the assessment as a basis for instructional adjustment rather than treat it only as an endpoint.`,
  F1: `Teachers reported a generally smooth beginning to Math Crew. Every participant had administered the addition and subtraction preassessment. The results showed a broad range of readiness across classrooms, reinforcing the reason for temporary skill groups. Several teachers described stronger performance in addition than in subtraction requiring regrouping. Word problems were particularly difficult, and English learners needed additional help with the academic language used in those tasks. These observations gave the team a more precise starting point than the broader iReady and SBA patterns alone.

Members also described practical successes in getting the groups underway. Students were moving between classrooms, teachers had explained expectations, and procedures such as name tags and lining up were helping transitions. Practicing the movement routines appeared to make students more comfortable with rotations. I drew attention to these examples because a predictable structure can support access to instruction. They were evidence that the arrangements were becoming workable, even though they did not yet demonstrate an academic effect.

Some procedures still required attention. Name tags were not used consistently, and expectations needed reinforcement across classrooms. Teachers wanted to continue developing language support for students who struggled with mathematics word problems. They also discussed the need to share information when a student required additional behavioral or safety support, including a risk of leaving the assigned area. Knowing these needs before a transition would help colleagues respond consistently. I asked how clearer communication and common expectations could address the concerns and recorded the areas requiring follow-up.

To encourage reflection, I invited teachers to compare the preassessment with their expectations and describe their first experiences teaching the groups. They said the results largely confirmed needs they had suspected from the earlier assessment information. Members also reported that planning together and agreeing on expectations increased their confidence. Sharing responsibility made implementation feel more coordinated than arranging groups independently. Several noted that students seemed to enjoy working with another teacher, which appeared to support engagement during the new routine.

The team had connected the Math Crew name with the school's existing character and growth-mindset framework. I acknowledged how that connection could help students understand and accept the new arrangement. I emphasized common language so that students would hear a consistent explanation of the groups' purpose and encounter familiar expectations in each room. Teachers felt that this shared approach was helping students manage transitions and begin taking responsibility for the routines. The discussion linked student participation with deliberate adult coordination rather than attribute a smooth start only to student enthusiasm.

I also encouraged proactive communication about academic, behavioral, and transition needs. Teachers described how exchanging this information helped them support students safely in another classroom. I affirmed the value of those practices and asked members to continue sharing relevant information as group membership changed. Flexible placement requires continuing communication, because a receiving teacher may need to know more than the student's assessment score. The group regarded these conversations as part of implementation rather than an unrelated administrative task.

In summarizing the meeting, I connected the preassessment patterns with the initial classroom observations. The team continued to see addition and subtraction with regrouping as an appropriate focus. Members identified engagement, clearer expectations, and improving transitions as early strengths, while language scaffolds and consistent procedures needed further attention. We agreed to continue Math Crew and refine the arrangements as more evidence became available.

Before the next meeting, teachers would collect student work, observations, and notes about responses to instruction. They would continue using the initial assessment to inform groups while watching for evidence that a placement or lesson needed to change. I would organize shared observations, support check-ins, and facilitate the next reflection. Our next discussion would examine early progress, possible changes in grouping or teaching, and the supports needed by students requiring additional scaffolding or enrichment.`,
  F2: `I opened the discussion by asking teachers what they were noticing as Math Crew continued. They reported that students generally responded positively to the rotation structure and seemed motivated by working with different teachers. Several described students looking forward to the group period. Some learners were already demonstrating strong addition-with-regrouping skills and appeared ready for more demanding work. Other teachers described success using visual models, gestures, and clearly sequenced steps to help students interpret and solve word problems. These examples gave the group concrete teaching practices to discuss rather than rely only on a general impression that students enjoyed the groups.

I connected these accounts to the purpose of intentional grouping and planning. The observations suggested that teachers were creating more accessible entry points while also identifying students who needed additional challenge. Members working with advanced groups said students were eager to attempt more complex problems. I acknowledged this as an indication that matching challenge to readiness was relevant across the range of learners. I summarized the shared pattern: students were responding at different levels, but teachers were finding ways to engage them with work appropriate to those levels. These remained early indicators from classroom experience, requiring continued review alongside learning evidence.

We then examined what needed refinement. Word problems remained difficult for some students, particularly when they involved comparison language or unfamiliar academic vocabulary. Teachers also described uncertainty about ones, tens, and hundreds during regrouping. I asked members to explain how they were responding. They discussed adding visual representations, modeling their reasoning, using gestures, and breaking a procedure into an explicit sequence. This allowed colleagues to connect a particular difficulty with a particular instructional adjustment and consider whether that adjustment might be useful in another group.

Placement was another area for review. Some students were progressing more quickly than expected, while others revealed needs that had not been obvious at the outset. Teachers discussed moving students between groups. I emphasized that such movement is central to a flexible arrangement: a change in placement can show that teachers are attending to current evidence rather than indicate that the initial plan was unsuccessful. We also considered pace. Students ready to advance needed continued challenge, while others required more time with foundational concepts. Toward the end of this discussion, the team agreed that the current cycle might need to run longer before we moved to a new focus.

I helped the group identify three connected areas for adjustment: the instructional supports within each group, the evidence used for placement changes, and the time available for learning. Framing these as normal parts of implementation made room for teachers to discuss difficulty openly. The purpose was to improve the fit between instruction and students rather than defend the original plan. Members could then consider revisions without treating every change as a departure from the agreed strategy.

During reflection, teachers again noted students' interest in rotations and the opportunity to learn with different adults. They described greater participation and a growing sense of responsibility for learning. They also identified explicit demonstrations, visuals, and hands-on materials as useful supports for regrouping and word problems. I acknowledged these observations while drawing attention to the team's role in making the supports available. Members were comparing experiences, modifying tasks, moving students when appropriate, and planning different levels of challenge. Their collaboration was becoming visible in instructional decisions rather than only in meeting attendance.

I summarized that teachers were beginning to plan deliberately for each group's needs. This included selecting tasks, deciding how much support to provide, and identifying opportunities for extension. Sharing those decisions helped the grade level maintain coherence while avoiding identical instruction for students with different starting points. I connected that work with shared responsibility for student learning and more equitable access to suitable instruction. The team would still need to study outcomes over time before making a broad judgment about effectiveness.

At the close, we agreed to extend the current cycle and continue monitoring progress. Teachers would keep planning targeted lessons using foundational practice, visual modeling, and enrichment tasks. They would reconsider placement as new evidence emerged and document observations or student work for the next meeting. I clarified what members should bring and restated my role in supporting coordination and reflection. The next discussion would review the effects of continued instruction, decide whether further refinements were needed, and consider readiness for a new content focus. These actions linked the meeting's observations with an explicit plan for the next stage of implementation.`,
  F3: `I facilitated a review of the postassessment after several weeks of Math Crew instruction. Teachers reported that most students had maintained or improved their performance. One account identified growth for 14 of 17 students, while another described 17 of 20 as maintaining or improving. Many students gained one or two performance points. The distinction between growth and maintaining a score needs to remain visible when interpreting those reports. The results nevertheless gave the team a concrete basis for examining progress rather than relying exclusively on students' enthusiasm for the groups.

We compared patterns across classrooms. Teachers described how grouping had allowed them to match instruction more closely to readiness. Students prepared for greater challenge worked on more complex tasks, while others received focused teaching in place value and regrouping. I highlighted the relationship between these instructional decisions and the progress teachers described. The evidence suggested promising changes in students' access and engagement, although a before-and-after comparison alone cannot isolate the effect of grouping from other instruction during the same period. The discussion therefore considered both the results and the way the strategy had been used.

We also examined students who showed less progress. Teachers identified continuing difficulties with foundational place value and a need for additional targeted support. Members raised concerns about the postassessment itself, including whether some items matched the instruction students had received and whether their complexity was appropriate for all groups. These concerns mattered because an assessment can be difficult for reasons beyond the particular skill the team intended to measure. I asked members to consider pacing and assessment design together rather than interpret every low result as the same learning problem.

The group agreed that future assessments should connect more precisely with the selected standards and skill progression. Teachers also wanted to check placement more often so students could move sooner when they needed different support or challenge. I emphasized that identifying these changes was part of responsive implementation. The evidence was useful because it helped the team reconsider its practice, not because it produced an uncomplicated success story. This discussion gave us specific improvements to carry into the next cycle.

In reflecting on the strategy, teachers said that a narrower range of readiness within each group made focused teaching more manageable. They could respond to misconceptions more quickly and select tasks suited to the learners present. Reviewing results with colleagues also increased their confidence in decisions about grouping, lesson planning, and task modification. I acknowledged how this shared analysis supported a collective responsibility for outcomes. The teachers were using information from several classrooms to guide their decisions rather than treating each classroom as a separate inquiry.

My summary brought together the positive performance patterns, continuing place-value needs, and assessment-alignment concerns. The group saw value in maintaining a flexible structure while improving how it assessed and responded to learning. We discussed moving most students toward multiplication and division, with continued small-group instruction in addition and subtraction for those who needed it. That direction allowed the team to advance the curriculum without assuming that every student was ready for the same next step.

For the next cycle, teachers agreed to revise the preassessment around specific standards and consider released or standards-based items. They would bring updated instructional plans and assessment tools to the next meeting. Continued monitoring would help determine whether students needed more foundational practice or were ready for greater challenge. I clarified these expectations and connected them with the concerns raised during the meeting. Our next discussion would examine how the revised assessment process supports grouping and instruction. The outcome was a plan to retain useful features of the strategy while addressing the limitations the evidence had revealed.`,
  G: `Work product 1: Two-page regrouping planning record. The team developed this document together to define its addition and subtraction focus. It connects lesson planning, task choices, and the interpretation of student understanding so that teachers can coordinate their work. The two pages serve as a common reference for the instructional cycle.

Work product 2: Initial planning minutes. This record describes the team's development of a preassessment and its decisions about the practical arrangements for small-group mathematics. It shows how assessment preparation and classroom logistics were considered together before implementation.

Work product 3: Scoring and grouping minutes. These notes record the discussion of initial assessment results, the formation of temporary instructional groups, and the allocation of teaching responsibilities. They connect student evidence with the organization of targeted instruction.

Work product 4: Two-page common assessment. The blank instrument represents the tasks used before and after instruction to examine students' understanding of regrouping. Its role is to support comparisons over the cycle and inform subsequent teaching, grouping, and reflection.

Work product 5: Class assessment summary. This record presents initial and later scores for 17 students without identifying them. The commentary associated with the third clip refers to it during discussion of student outcomes and the level of assessment demand.

Work product 6: Additional postassessment discussed with clip 3. This blank assessment is referenced in the discussion of whether the tasks matched the instructional focus and expected rigor. Together, the products document planning, implementation, and reflection; their descriptions do not substitute for independently checking the underlying evidence.`,
  H: `The commentary describes several recordings that were unavailable for inspection. This section therefore contains neither a replacement video nor a fabricated account of recorded dialogue.`,
  I1: `The first clip is described as opening with a welcome and a statement that the team would review the beginning of Math Crew implementation (0:00-0:28). I then revisit the working agreements: attentive participation, preparation, openness to other perspectives, and maintaining the focus. I ask members to confirm those agreements before proceeding (0:29-0:53). This opening is intended to establish both a respectful way of working and a shared reason for the meeting. The emphasis is on collective responsibility for the inquiry rather than separate reports from individual classrooms.

I next connect the discussion with earlier evidence and decisions. The commentary references our SBA and iReady review and the selection of addition and subtraction with regrouping as a prerequisite for grade-level mathematics (0:55-1:26). I restate the instructional problem as a need for more targeted, differentiated teaching in foundational numeracy so students can progress toward grade-level expectations (1:26-1:40). I also revisit flexible grouping through Math Crew and ask whether the team remains ready to continue with the approach (1:40-2:16). Returning to these decisions keeps the relationship between adult practice and student learning visible across meetings.

The third clip is described as beginning with another explicit statement of purpose. After several weeks of implementation, the team would examine postassessment evidence and decide what should happen next (0:00-0:18). I frame the conversation as learning about the strategy's results and using that learning to guide subsequent action. This repeated opening links the meetings as stages in an ongoing inquiry rather than unrelated conversations. Across the two examples, purpose-setting involves identifying the current stage of the work while retaining the same instructional concern. The timestamps are references in the written commentary; the recordings themselves were not available to verify the described interactions.`,
  I2: `Across the three described clips, the discussion moves from initial assessment information to classroom implementation evidence and then to postassessment results. My intention is to help teachers interpret what the evidence suggests about instruction, not simply report scores. That progression gives the data a role in revising practice and deciding what support students need next.

In clip 1, I invite members to describe what they noticed on the assessment used for grouping (2:17-2:39). Teachers identify subtraction with regrouping and word problems as particular difficulties (2:44-4:12). I draw together observations across classrooms and identify the language demands of word problems as an equity concern for English learners (4:13-4:41). This synthesis helps turn individual accounts into a shared view of the instructional needs the team should monitor.

Clip 2 shifts to what teachers are seeing while the groups operate. I begin by asking whether those observations suggest any adjustments (0:00-0:09). Members describe students ready for additional challenge or a different group (0:12-0:31), and students struggling with comparison language in word problems (0:34-0:47). They explain their use of visuals, gestures, and sequenced problem-solving steps (0:49-1:20). Other contributions concern students' motivation when tasks provide an appropriate level of challenge (1:27-1:58).

I connect these examples with the purpose of flexible grouping. Different responses help teachers identify readiness and adapt instruction. Later in the clip, I explain that movement among groups is expected as students develop or reveal further needs (4:27-4:45). This frames adjustment as part of the agreed strategy rather than evidence that implementation is inconsistent.

In clip 3, teachers discuss postassessment figures, including 14 of 17 and 17 of 20 students described as maintaining or improving (0:19-1:21). They report one- or two-point increases for many students and identify place-value needs among those with less progress (0:41-1:21). I summarize the broadly positive pattern while acknowledging continuing support needs (2:48-3:01). The discussion considers both promising results and limitations, including the meaning of maintaining a score. These descriptions show the intended movement from evidence to interpretation, but the unavailable recordings prevent independent confirmation of the interactions and the precise language used.`,
  I3: `In the first clip, I begin by confirming the team's common understanding of the problem and selected approach before turning to assessment analysis (0:29-2:16). After teachers describe initial results, I connect their accounts by identifying shared needs in subtraction with regrouping and the language of word problems (4:13-4:41). This summary is intended to help members agree on what the information suggests and what deserves attention during implementation. Agreement is grounded in the observations members have contributed.

The second clip concerns decisions about teaching and group placement. Teachers discuss moving students when their readiness changes (0:12-0:31 and 2:27-3:27). I reinforce that changing groups is appropriate when current evidence indicates a different need (4:27-4:45). Presenting movement as an expected feature of the approach supports continued use of the strategy while leaving room for revision. It helps the team avoid treating the original grouping plan as fixed.

In the third clip, consensus develops around the interpretation of results and the next instructional direction. Teachers report both progress and continuing needs. I ask what the results suggest we should do next (3:37-3:41). A member explains that a narrower readiness range makes focused support easier (3:43-4:10), then suggests making the next preassessment more specific (4:14-4:26). I summarize a direction in which many students move to new skills while others continue receiving small-group or push-in assistance (4:28-4:46).

Before the clip ends, I restate the next steps and relate them to the assessment discussion and teacher reflections (4:46-4:59). The intention is to confirm that the plan represents the group's contributions and addresses different readiness levels. Across the meetings, synthesis and confirmation help connect individual observations with collective action. A written description of consensus still has limits: the recordings were not available to examine whether each member explicitly agreed or how disagreement was handled. The commentary should therefore be read as an account of the facilitation, with its evidence status kept visible.`,
  I4: `Across the three described clips, I use questions, acknowledgement, and summaries to support teacher participation while keeping the instructional purpose visible. Different interpretations can contribute to a fuller understanding of students rather than simply slow agreement.

In clip 1, I revisit agreements about respect and openness to other perspectives (0:29-0:52). I invite each teacher to contribute observations (2:27-2:40). When members identify different concerns, including subtraction difficulty and language access for English learners, I connect them in the summary rather than select only one as relevant (4:13-4:41). This gives several kinds of classroom evidence a place in the shared interpretation.

Clip 2 opens with a focused question about changes suggested by current observations (0:00-0:09). I use follow-up questions to clarify contributions. For example, when a teacher describes a group as especially difficult, I clarify whether the reference concerns the level of challenge and the support required (1:50-1:57). Later, I summarize the different supports groups need and the expected movement between them (4:27-5:00). These moves are intended to maintain a useful instructional focus without dismissing teachers' experiences.

In clip 3, I identify the purpose as reviewing results and deciding next steps (0:00-0:18). I listen to concerns about assessment demand and students with limited progress (1:32-2:46), acknowledge those concerns, and also note the broadly positive pattern reported by teachers (2:48-3:01). I then ask the group to consider subsequent action (3:37-3:41). The account illustrates an effort to balance recognition of strengths with attention to unresolved needs. Because the clips were unavailable, the written commentary cannot by itself verify how equitably speaking time was distributed or how participants experienced these facilitation choices.`,
  I5: `Across the meetings, I connect evidence with action by posing questions, summarizing proposals, and checking the group's direction. The purpose is to make subsequent steps emerge from shared discussion rather than assign them independently after the meeting.

In clip 1, after the preassessment discussion, I ask whether groups have started and how frequently they are meeting (4:44-4:58). This links the initial data with the practical use of the strategy and establishes that implementation itself needs monitoring.

In clip 2, members propose changes in placement, additional visuals and gestures, and renewed attention to place-value concepts (0:12-1:21 and 2:27-3:52). I acknowledge these suggestions and explain that changes in grouping and teaching belong within the flexible approach (4:27-4:45). This supports the team's willingness to adapt the plan as students respond.

Clip 3 focuses on the postassessment and the next content priorities. I ask how we should respond both to students needing reinforcement and to those ready to advance (3:34-3:41). Teachers support continued small-group addition and subtraction practice for some learners while most begin multiplication and division (4:27-4:44). They also identify a need for assessments that more precisely match the intended standards and reveal students' understanding (4:15-4:25).

I close by summarizing these decisions so members leave with a shared account of responsibilities and direction. The described next steps connect teacher dialogue with student evidence and the goal of access to grade-level mathematics. The written commentary supplies the sequence and timestamps; it does not replace direct review of the recordings, which were unavailable for inspection.`,
  J1: `My leadership in Cycle 2 centered on establishing the conditions for a community of practice to move from planning into instruction and reflection. I prepared structures and shared resources that helped members focus on the problem of students' understanding of addition and subtraction with regrouping. The aim was to make targeted small-group teaching a coordinated inquiry rather than a set of unrelated classroom activities. Preparation mattered because members needed both an instructional purpose and workable arrangements before they could study students' responses.

After the initial planning session, I facilitated another meeting devoted to the common preassessment and implementation logistics. The team developed the assessment together and clarified a regular weekly period for small-group instruction, as recorded in work product 2. Establishing the tool, schedule, and content focus in advance reduced the number of practical questions teachers had to resolve separately. It allowed the subsequent discussion to concentrate on students' needs and the teaching those needs required.

I did not attend the following data-analysis meeting. The minutes nevertheless show that members continued the work: they scored the initial assessment together, compared patterns, and formed groups around the identified skills, as documented in work product 3. This is relevant evidence of continuity and shared responsibility. The team could act without waiting for me to direct each step. It suggests that the earlier planning supported distributed leadership, although it would be an overstatement to attribute every independent decision solely to my facilitation.

The written account of clip 1 describes teachers referring to common instructional practices during early implementation. The shared assessment, work product 4, supplied initial and later measures of understanding. In the account of clip 3, teachers use the class score summary in work product 5 to discuss outcomes and question assessment demand and alignment. These connections among tools, meetings, and instruction show how the community intended to monitor early results and revise practice. The recordings were unavailable for independent review, so the written descriptions remain the evidence available for those interactions.

Advance preparation and clear structure were strengths in my facilitation. Planning for both instruction and logistics helped members continue the inquiry, including at a meeting I could not attend. The minutes and commentary describe teachers analyzing results, grouping students, and discussing instruction in relation to the same goal. The aggregate report indicates that 72 of 85 students, approximately 85%, maintained or improved their scores. For students who gained one or two points on an eight-point assessment, those gains represent 12.5 or 25 percentage points of the available scale. Maintaining and improving should be distinguished, and these changes should not be described as proof that the strategy alone caused growth. They do, however, provide evidence for the team's reflection.

A second strength was supporting an atmosphere in which teachers could discuss difficulties honestly. The account of clip 3 includes concern that a postassessment demanded more than some students had encountered in instruction. We treated that concern as a question about alignment rather than an occasion to blame a teacher or student. Members' willingness to raise the issue suggests a degree of trust and shared ownership. It also gave the group useful information for revising future assessments and deciding what students still needed.

When I asked for feedback, members described the meetings as organized, collaborative, and supportive of candid reflection. Several expressed interest in having me facilitate future discussions. I value that feedback, while recognizing that positive comments should be considered alongside observed practice and specific examples. It encouraged me to retain the structures that were useful and to improve the moments where my facilitation could be clearer. Creating conditions for professional learning includes making it possible to examine misalignment without defensiveness and turn that examination into an instructional decision.

My clearest growth area was synthesizing contributions while a discussion was still unfolding. At times, I found it difficult to retain the details from several teachers and immediately articulate the pattern connecting them. Reviewing the written record and my account of the recordings helped me identify places where a pause, a paraphrase, or visible notes could have improved accuracy. I want to slow the conversation deliberately when needed so that a summary represents the contributions rather than merely the comments I remember most readily.

This is also an equity concern. Listening carefully, checking interpretations, and making ideas visible can prevent the most confident voices from determining the group's conclusion too early. Documentation has a related role: the minutes preserved decisions and allowed the inquiry to continue between meetings. In future facilitation, I would use shared notes during the conversation to capture patterns, questions, and next steps as they emerge. These changes would build on my preparation strengths while improving real-time synthesis, representation of different perspectives, and clarity about collective decisions.`,
  J2: `I sought to demonstrate professionalism and integrity by keeping the discussion respectful, centered on student evidence, and open to shared decisions. At the additional planning meeting, I organized the agenda so members could contribute to assessment design and instructional arrangements, as shown in work product 2. We used the common student information to guide decisions rather than treat an individual's preference as sufficient justification. This supported a professional learning environment in which colleagues could explain their reasoning and influence the plan.

The working agreements also supported equity in participation. Openness to different perspectives, focused discussion, and respectful interaction were identified in the planning agenda and revisited in later agendas. These agreements were intended to give different instructional experiences a place in the conversation while maintaining the common goal. Their value depends on how they are enacted, including whether members' ideas are heard and accurately represented.

During implementation discussions, I encouraged candid examination of practice and acknowledged teachers' professional knowledge. The account of clip 3 describes concern that a postassessment, identified in work product 6, exceeded the demand of the instruction. I guided the discussion toward the relationship between teaching and assessment and the changes needed next. Focusing on that relationship allowed the team to consider a difficulty without assigning blame. It illustrates the kind of trust and professional responsibility I wanted to support: use a concern as evidence for learning and improvement. The written account describes this intention and action, while the unavailable recording limits independent verification.`,
  J3: `School conditions shaped both the implementation plan and my facilitation. Limited instructional time made it necessary to use existing arrangements carefully. The team established a dependable weekly period for small groups that fit the school schedule and mathematics priorities. This helped make the strategy feasible without displacing the core instruction it was intended to support.

Grade-level collaboration time was another important condition. Teams have scheduled opportunities to meet twice a month while students attend physical education. Because I teach a different grade, I could not attend the second community meeting held during that period. I supported the teachers' decision to use the time rather than ask them to wait for me. They wanted to score the preassessment and form groups, and their initiative indicated growing ownership of the inquiry.

The school's emphasis on collaboration and shared leadership helped make that possible. Members continued analysis and planning independently. My response was to ensure that the earlier structures were clear and then support coordination and reflection in subsequent meetings. I did not need to reclaim control simply because the team had progressed without my presence. Their ability to continue was relevant to the community's development.

Teachers also brought different levels of experience with regrouping instruction. I kept the discussion connected to common student evidence so those differences could become resources for problem-solving rather than reasons to label colleagues or students negatively. This supported a shared responsibility for learners across classrooms. Together, the scheduling arrangements, collaborative culture, and varied instructional experience required facilitation that combined preparation with flexibility. The work had to fit the setting while still allowing members to shape and sustain the inquiry themselves.`,
  J4: `My next professional learning goals follow from the strengths and difficulties identified in the meeting records and reflection. First, I want to improve how I synthesize contributions during a discussion. Preparation helped the community work productively, but I need to pause more consistently, paraphrase ideas, and record them where everyone can see them. I plan to use structured shared notes and deliberate pauses so patterns can be identified accurately and different voices remain represented in the summary.

Second, I want to develop shared leadership more intentionally. The team already demonstrated that it could continue the inquiry without me. I can build on that by inviting members to lead parts of meetings, guide a data discussion, or summarize the group's thinking. I will ask about their interests and prepare agendas that distribute these responsibilities. This should deepen participation and ownership while giving members opportunities to practice facilitation themselves.

I also plan to use documentation more actively during meetings, capturing decisions and next steps as the conversation develops rather than reconstruct them afterward. Brief opportunities for participant feedback will help me learn whether those changes improve clarity and inclusion. These goals connect directly to the cycle: retain useful planning structures, strengthen listening and synthesis, and create more opportunities for the community to lead its own work.`,
};

const details = {
  A1: 'The shared presentation also allowed grade levels to learn about approaches outside their own team. Seeing another group\'s problem, strategy, and evidence could broaden the conversation about a common reading goal. This transparency was a practical feature of the process, alongside the more formal expectation that teams present their results.',
  A3: 'The focus therefore connects immediate school improvement with later learning opportunities.',
  A4: 'Their agreement established a shared starting commitment for the planned inquiry.',
  A5: 'These descriptions concern representation within this particular school community.',
  A6: 'That colleague relationship shapes how I invite participation and discuss evidence.',
  A7: 'The community will revisit those expectations as evidence becomes available.',
  B: 'Decision record: Capture the problem statement, the reason for the selected approach, and any implementation questions still requiring evidence. Make these notes available to all participants so the next meeting can continue from a common account of the discussion.',
  C: 'The comparison of strategies also helped distinguish a teaching routine from a grouping structure. Number Talks could provide regular opportunities to reason and explain, while flexible grouping could organize more targeted instruction around different skill needs. The team did not need to regard one as universally better. It needed to decide which would serve as the primary response to this particular problem in the first cycle.\n\nThe shared preassessment was an important part of that decision. Broad iReady placement information had identified a concern, but the regrouping tasks would give teachers more immediate evidence about the concepts they intended to teach. Discussing the assessment together also helped members establish a common interpretation of what students would be asked to demonstrate. That preparation would support later discussion of group placement and progress.\n\nThe implementation plan retained both consistency and teacher judgment. Shared time, language, and models would make the arrangement coherent for students moving among classrooms. Within that structure, teachers could select scaffolds and extensions appropriate to the group. The team would revisit those choices after seeing student work, so the initial plan was a starting point for inquiry rather than a permanent assignment of students or instructional tasks.',
  D1: 'The focus remained on changes the teachers could make together.',
  D8: 'This process links each meeting with the instruction occurring between meetings.',
  E1: 'The record should distinguish a procedure that is operating smoothly from evidence that students have mastered the mathematics. Both kinds of information are useful at this early stage.',
  E2: 'Members will explain the evidence behind a proposed move so that group changes remain connected with learning needs and are understood across the team.',
  E3: 'Keep the interpretation connected with the skills actually assessed and the instruction students received during the cycle.',
  F1: 'The discussion also reinforced why logistical details belong in an instructional meeting during a new implementation. Students need to know where to go and what to expect before they can use the learning time well. Consistent procedures allow teachers to focus more of that time on the different academic needs identified by the assessment.',
  F2: 'The extension of the cycle was therefore a response to evidence, not simply a change of calendar. Teachers wanted enough time for students to develop understanding before drawing conclusions from the later assessment. At the same time, students ready for greater challenge would continue receiving differentiated tasks rather than wait for every group to move together.',
  F3: 'The meeting also clarified that progress should be examined within groups as well as across the grade. A generally positive total can coexist with students who still need substantial help. Teachers wanted the next plan to preserve that distinction. The assessment discussion was similarly specific: greater rigor is useful when it reveals the intended learning, but a task that does not align with the taught skill can make interpretation harder. These considerations informed both the proposed new content and the continued foundational support.',
  G: 'Each record has a distinct place in the sequence from initial planning to the review of results.',
  I1: 'The opening also provides an opportunity to renew agreement rather than assume that a previous decision still represents everyone\'s view. Confirming the strategy before analysis makes later discussion of adjustments easier to locate within the shared plan. It gives the team a common reference for distinguishing a change in implementation from a change in the overall purpose.',
  I2: 'The three stages use different kinds of information for different decisions. Initial results help identify where to begin, classroom observations help teachers adjust while instruction is underway, and later results help them consider what to retain or change. Keeping that sequence visible supports a continuing inquiry instead of treating one assessment result as a complete evaluation of the approach.',
  I3: 'The summaries also make the basis for the next decision visible. Members can hear how their comments have been combined and can correct an interpretation before it becomes the team\'s plan. That checking process is particularly relevant when students need different next steps.',
  I4: 'Maintaining focus therefore involves both inviting contributions and connecting them with the purpose. Acknowledging a concern is followed by asking what it means for instruction, assessment, or the next decision the team needs to make.',
  I5: 'The action sequence includes implementation frequency, instructional supports, placement decisions, new content, and assessment design. Each is connected with evidence discussed by the team rather than introduced as an unrelated task.',
  J1: 'The group\'s continued work during my absence also challenged the idea that facilitation requires constant control. Shared tools and a clear purpose can allow members to lead, while the facilitator helps maintain continuity across meetings. I want to build on that experience by making opportunities for member leadership more deliberate.',
  J3: 'Using existing time also respected teachers\' responsibilities and allowed their motivation to carry the work forward within the school\'s established schedule.',
  J4: 'Member feedback will help me judge the usefulness of these changes.'
};
for (const [id, text] of Object.entries(details)) {
  const paragraphs = module.exports[id].split('\n\n');
  if (paragraphs.length === 1) paragraphs[0] += ' ' + text;
  else paragraphs.splice(paragraphs.length - 1, 0, text);
  module.exports[id] = paragraphs.join('\n\n');
}

module.exports.C += '\n\nThe recorded decisions provide a shared reference for checking what was implemented and what the next discussion needs to resolve.';
